+          "quote": "Let $k\\ge1$, $d\\ge1$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be the right-hand side of a first-order initial-value problem on an open set $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$, using the convention of [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]. A **constant-step $k$-step linear multistep method** has real coefficients $\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_k,\\beta_0,\\ldots,\\beta_k$ with $\\alpha_k\\ne0$ and advances values by\n\n$$\\sum_{j=0}^k\\alpha_jY_{n+j}=h\\sum_{j=0}^k\\beta_j f(t_{n+j},Y_{n+j}),\\qquad t_{n+j}=t_n+jh,\\quad h>0.$$\n\nMultiplying all coefficients by the same nonzero scalar leaves the method unchanged; one may normalize $\\alpha_k=1$. Its **characteristic polynomials** are\n\n$$\\rho(\\zeta)=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\alpha_j\\zeta^j,\\qquad \\sigma(\\zeta)=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\beta_j\\zeta^j.$$\n\nFor an exact solution $y$ with $p+1$ continuous derivatives, the **unscaled local residual** at $t_n$ is\n\n$$\\tau_n(h;y)=\\sum_{j=0}^k\\alpha_jy(t_n+jh)-h\\sum_{j=0}^k\\beta_jy'(t_n+jh).$$\n\nThe method has **order at least $p$**, where $p\\ge1$ is an integer, if this residual is $O(h^{p+1})$ uniformly for admitted starting times in every fixed compact interval on which the indicated derivatives are bounded. It is **consistent** if it has order at least one. Equivalently, with the above coefficient convention, consistency is $\\rho(1)=0$ and $\\rho'(1)=\\sigma(1)$.\n\nThe **root condition** is that every complex root of $\\rho$ has modulus at most one and every root of modulus one has algebraic multiplicity one. The method is **zero-stable** if for each $T>0$ there is a constant $M_T$ independent of $N$ such that every solution of the homogeneous recurrence $\\sum_j\\alpha_jY_{n+j}=0$ on the uniform grid $h=T/N$ satisfies\n\n$$\\max_{0\\le n\\le N}\\|Y_n\\|_\\infty\\le M_T\\max_{0\\le j<k}\\|Y_j\\|_\\infty.$$\n\nHere $\\|x\\|_\\infty=\\max_{1\\le i\\le d}|x_i|$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$. The root-condition equivalence for zero-stability is established separately.",
           "uses": [
             "1.1"
           ]
