+          "quote": "Input $n\\ge1$, a nonempty family of Hermitian $n\\times n$ matrices enclosed by complex rectangles, a point matrix $V\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}$, a real diagonal matrix $\\Theta\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$, and proposed nonnegative bounds $\\delta,\\rho,M_A,M_\\Theta,\\tau$. Treat point data as exact dyadics and the family as any subset of its enclosure.\n\n1. With outward interval evaluation, enclose $V^*V-I$ and every $AV-V\\Theta$ for the full family. Form Frobenius upper bounds $d,r$ for these two matrices. Also form Frobenius upper bounds $m_A$ for every $A$ in the family and $m_\\Theta$ for $\\Theta$. If any enclosure or square-root operation is non-finite, return `inconclusive`.\n\n2. Verify by outward scalar comparisons that $d\\le\\delta<1$, $r\\le\\rho$, $m_A\\le M_A$, $m_\\Theta\\le M_\\Theta$, and that $1-\\sqrt{1-\\delta}\\le\\tau$. If any comparison is undecided or false, return `inconclusive` with the failed check. Otherwise outward-compute a finite upper bound $\\bar\\eta\\ge\\rho+(M_A+M_\\Theta)\\tau$. For each increasingly sorted diagonal entry $\\theta_{(k)}$, outward-compute finite endpoints $\\ell_k\\le\\theta_{(k)}-\\bar\\eta$ and $u_k\\ge\\theta_{(k)}+\\bar\\eta$, and set $I_k=[\\ell_k,u_k]$. If the radius bound or any final endpoint cannot be enclosed finitely, return `inconclusive`; otherwise return `certified` with radius bound $\\bar\\eta$ and intervals $I_1,\\ldots,I_n$.\n\nThe checks use the Frobenius norm as an upper bound for the operator 2-norm. An inconclusive check makes no statement about the true spectrum.",
